Short answer
- Use an optimistic UI on the client (render the message immediately with a “pending” state and a temporary id).
- Send the message to the server and get a quick receipt/acknowledgement (temp id accepted) — do not block the UI.
- Persist reliably on the backend asynchronously (save to MongoDB, publish to RabbitMQ or queue), then notify clients when the message is persisted/delivered (update the message status).
- Handle errors with try/catch and notify the client to mark a message failed / allow retry.
- Batch writes if load requires it (you already use batch size 100; tune up/down based on latency vs memory).

Code examples (async error handling included)

Server: accept message, ack immediately, persist async and notify on success/failure
```javascript
// server.js (Socket.io + MongoDB + optional RabbitMQ)
const io = require('socket.io')(3000);
const MessageService = require('./MessageService'); // see below

io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  socket.on('chatMessage', async (payload) => {
    // payload: { tempId, text, senderId, roomId }
    // 1) Immediate acknowledgement so UI stays responsive
    socket.emit('messageReceived', { tempId: payload.tempId });

    // 2) Persist asynchronously (non-blocking). MessageService.saveMessage returns saved message or throws
    try {
      const saved = await MessageService.saveMessage(payload);
      // 3) Broadcast persisted message (with DB id and timestamp) to room
      io.to(payload.roomId).emit('messageSaved', {
        tempId: payload.tempId,
        id: saved._id,
        text: saved.text,
        senderId: saved.senderId,
        roomId: saved.roomId,
        timestamp: saved.timestamp,
      });
    } catch (err) {
      // 4) Notify the sender that save failed so UI can mark failed and allow retry
      socket.emit('messageSaveFailed', { tempId: payload.tempId, error: err.message });
    }
  });

  socket.on('joinRoom', (roomId) => socket.join(roomId));
});
```

MessageService example with async error handling and acknowledgement (based on your async preference)
```javascript
// MessageService.js
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const amqp = require('amqplib'); // optional: use RabbitMQ for decoupling
const Message = require('./models/Message'); // mongoose model

async function saveMessage(payload) {
  try {
    // persist to DB
    const newMessage = new Message({
      text: payload.text,
      senderId: payload.senderId,
      roomId: payload.roomId,
      timestamp: new Date()
    });
    const saved = await newMessage.save();

    // optional: publish to queue for analytics / further processing
    try {
      const connection = await amqp.connect('amqp://localhost');
      const channel = await connection.createChannel();
      const queue = 'chat-messages';
      await channel.assertQueue(queue, { durable: true });
      channel.sendToQueue(queue, Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(saved)), { persistent: true });
      // optionally close channel/connection or reuse connection in production
    } catch (queueErr) {
      console.error('Failed to publish to queue:', queueErr);
      // Do not fail the save overall just because queue publish failed
    }

    return saved;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error saving message:', error);
    throw error; // caller will notify client about failure
  }
}

module.exports = { saveMessage };
```

Client: optimistic UI, handle acks and final saved/delivered statuses
```javascript
// React-ish pseudocode
// state.messages = [{ id, tempId, text, senderId, roomId, timestamp, status: 'pending'|'sent'|'failed' }]

function sendMessage(text, roomId, senderId) {
  const tempId = `t-${Date.now()}`;
  // 1) Add optimistic message
  setMessages(prev => [...prev, { tempId, text, senderId, roomId, timestamp: Date.now(), status: 'pending' }]);

  // 2) Send to server
  socket.emit('chatMessage', { tempId, text, senderId, roomId });
}

// 3) Handle quick ack (optional)
socket.on('messageReceived', ({ tempId }) => {
  // optionally update UI to show "received by server"
  setMessages(prev => prev.map(m => m.tempId === tempId ? { ...m, status: 'received' } : m));
});

// 4) Handle final persisted message -> replace temp entry with DB id & mark sent
socket.on('messageSaved', (saved) => {
  setMessages(prev => prev.map(m => {
    if (m.tempId === saved.tempId) {
      return {
        ...m,
        id: saved.id,
        timestamp: saved.timestamp,
        status: 'sent',
        tempId: undefined
      };
    }
    return m;
  }));
});

// 5) Handle save failures
socket.on('messageSaveFailed', ({ tempId, error }) => {
  setMessages(prev => prev.map(m => m.tempId === tempId ? { ...m, status: 'failed', error } : m));
  // UI can show retry button that calls sendMessage again with same text
});
```

Notes, tradeoffs and tuning
- Synchronous save-before-broadcast (save then emit before ack) guarantees listeners see only persisted messages (you showed this in your sample). But it blocks confirmation to the client until DB write completes and can increase client-perceived latency.
- Asynchronous persistence with an immediate ack + later persisted notification (the pattern above) keeps the UI smooth and responsive while preserving reliability: persist to DB and queue in background and notify when done.
- Use RabbitMQ (or other durable queue) to decouple persistence/analytics/replication and avoid slowing the message path. Your earlier architecture notes (MongoDB + RabbitMQ) fit well here.
- Batch writes can reduce DB load (you already tried batch size 100). Tune batch size by measuring end-to-end latency and memory usage — increase batch to reduce DB ops, but larger batches increase latency for individual messages.
- Always catch and log errors as shown; notify the client on failures so the UI can show retry options.
- For eventual consistency of read receipts / delivery statuses, emit status updates from message consumers (or persistence step) to the room (e.g., messageSaved, messageDelivered, messageRead).

Summary
- Render immediately on the client (optimistic), send the message, acknowledge quickly, persist asynchronously (with try/catch), and emit a final persisted/delivered event to update UI state. This combination keeps the UI smooth while providing reliable persistence and clear error handling.

Use structured local handling plus centralized/global monitoring. Key techniques from your excerpts:

- Wrap async work in try/catch and handle or rethrow errors so they don’t become unhandled promise rejections.
- Log errors with a logging library (Winston) for consistent records.
- Notify your team or an external service for critical failures (example used axios).
- For API handlers, return after sending a response to avoid "Cannot set headers after they are sent".
- Emit an error event back to the client (socket.emit) when appropriate.
- Use a global handler for unhandled rejections (process.on('unhandledRejection')) to catch anything missed.
- Use finally (or a finally-equivalent) to clean up resources or hide spinners regardless of success/failure.
- For batch/parallel work, use Promise.all and batch operations to keep things efficient and easier to handle errors in aggregate.

